WEBVTT

00:00:00.140 --> 00:00:09.950
<v Ira Glass>Glen's not being mean when he mentions his other family, the one back in Africa. He's only seven, and adopted, and black, living with Cate and her husband, who are white.
<v Ira Glass>格伦提到他在非洲的另一个家庭时并不是恶意的。他才七岁，是个被收养的黑人男孩，和凯特及其丈夫住在一起，他们是白人。

00:00:09.950 --> 00:00:54.970
<v Cate>It comes up if he's punished, for instance. The best example I can think of is he's not allowed to cross the street by himself at seven. And he went over to a neighbor's house and then he came back by himself. And I kind of saw him do that. I knew he was looking, but I followed through. This is against the rules. And so when he came in the house he was sent to his room. And it's upstairs. And he sort of stomped up the stairs. "I'm going to leave. I'm out of here. I'm out of here. I'm going back to my family in Africa. I'm out of this family." Because in Africa, he was a prince, you have to understand.
<v Cate>比如他受罚的时候，这件事就会被提起。我能想到的最好的例子是，他七岁时还不被允许独自过马路。他跑到邻居家，又独自回来了。我其实看见他这么做了。我知道他在观察我，但我还是照章办事。这是违反规定的。所以他进屋后被关进了自己的房间。房间在楼上。他跺着脚上了楼。“我要离开。我受够了。我走人了。我要回非洲找我的家人。我要离开这个家庭。”因为你必须明白，在非洲，他曾经是个王子。

00:00:54.970 --> 00:01:01.450
<v Cate>And he's stomping up the stairs and I sort of said to him, "They have rules in Africa."
<v Cate>他跺着脚上楼时，我对他说：“非洲也有规矩。”

00:01:01.450 --> 00:01:10.870
<v Cate>And he said, "Yeah, but they don't have roads in Africa. And so I wouldn't be grounded in Africa."
<v Cate>他说：“是啊，但非洲没有公路。所以我在非洲才不会被禁足呢。”

00:01:10.870 --> 00:01:31.620
<v Ira Glass>Now this departs from reality in a number of ways. Number one, of course, there are roads in Africa. Number two, Glen wasn't adopted from Africa. He's not a prince. He was adopted from Chicago, which he knows very well. He has other parents who he could fantasize about, other parents who he could actually meet. But he doesn't want that.
<v Ira Glass>现在，这在很多方面都脱离了现实。第一，当然，非洲是有公路的。第二，格伦并不是从非洲收养的。他也不是什么王子。他是从芝加哥收养的，这一点他很清楚。他有其他的父母可以幻想，其他的父母他实际上是可以去见的。但他不想那样。

00:01:31.620 --> 00:01:42.410
<v Cate>Not yet. I ask that many times and I have offered to help him find them. He's not ready for that.
<v Cate>还没到时候。我问过他很多次，也主动提出帮他寻找他们。但他还没准备好。

00:01:42.410 --> 00:01:49.450
<v Ira Glass>It's so interesting that it's not a desire for the actual parents, it's a desire for perfect parents.
<v Ira Glass>很有趣的是，这并不是对亲生父母的渴望，而是对完美父母的渴望。

00:01:49.450 --> 00:02:00.040
<v Cate>Fantasy is pretty wonderful. If you were a prince in Africa you wouldn't want to give it up either.
<v Cate>幻想是件非常美妙的事情。如果你在非洲是个王子，你也不会想放弃这一切的。

00:02:00.040 --> 00:02:32.100
<v Ira Glass>In 1908, Sigmund Freud wrote an article with the disturbing name "Family Romances," in which he pointed out how common it is for children to fantasize, at some point or another, that the parents that they live with are not their real parents. Their real parents are out there, somewhere, and are perfect. Aristocrats, Freud says. It's comforting compared with the flaws of any real parents. And if you are somebody whose real parents aren't around anymore, really, how will you choose to imagine them? When do you want reality and when do you want fantasy?
<v Ira Glass>1908年，西格蒙德·弗洛伊德写了一篇标题令人不安的文章，叫《家庭罗曼史》，他在文中指出，孩子在某个时刻幻想自己现在的父母并非亲生父母是非常普遍的现象。他们的亲生父母在某个地方，而且是完美的。弗洛伊德说，是贵族。与任何现实父母的缺点相比，这令人感到安慰。如果你是一个亲生父母已不在身边的人，你真的会选择如何去想象他们呢？你什么时候想要现实，什么时候又想要幻想？

00:02:32.100 --> 00:02:46.570
<v Ira Glass>Well, today on our radio show, we bring you stories of children put into that position. From WBEZ Chicago and Public Radio International, it's This American Life. I'm Ira Glass. Today, stories from the Missing Parents Bureau. We bring you four case examples.
<v Ira Glass>今天，在我们的广播节目中，我们为您带来处于这种境地的孩子们的故事。这里是来自芝加哥WBEZ和国际公共广播电台的《美国生活》。我是艾拉·格拉斯。今天的故事来自“失踪父母调查局”。我们为您带来四个案例。

00:02:46.570 --> 00:03:40.110
<v Ira Glass>Case One, Better Left to the Imagination. Why mothers who are impregnated with samples from sperm banks might choose to know less about the donors rather than more. Case Two, Tell It to the Void, in which we have these funny, moving letters the mom of a 13 year old has written to the kid's absent dad, letters she writes even though she has no idea where to send them. Act Three, I'm an Orphan; Don't Tell My Mom. Starlee Kine remembers back in the day when everybody wanted to be an orphan. Act Four, Runaway Mom. It's the old story. Baby meets mom. Baby loses mom. Baby asks after mom. Adoptive parents wonder what to say about mom. That story from Dan Savage. Stay with us.
<v Ira Glass>案例一，留给想象更好。为什么通过精子库受孕的母亲们可能选择少了解捐赠者而不是多了解。案例二，告诉虚空，我们收录了一位13岁孩子的母亲写给她缺席父亲的感人信件，尽管她根本不知道寄到哪里。第三幕，我是孤儿，别告诉妈妈。斯塔莉·凯恩回忆起每个人都想成为孤儿的旧时光。第四幕，逃跑的妈妈。这是个老生常谈的故事。宝宝遇见妈妈。宝宝失去妈妈。宝宝询问妈妈。养父母在想关于妈妈该说些什么。这是丹·萨维奇带来的故事。请继续收听。

00:03:40.110 --> 00:03:43.690
<v Ira Glass>Act One, Better Left to the Imagination.
<v Ira Glass>第一幕，留给想象更好。

00:03:43.690 --> 00:04:10.650
<v Ira Glass>If you want to understand how much kids and the parents left behind think about an absent parent, and what is that they do think about, consider what happens when kids are born to infertile couples, or to single moms, with sperm gotten from a sperm bank. Most sperm banks provide all sorts of information about their donors. And parents getting this sperm can actually choose how much they want to find out. How much they want their kid to find out. And how much they'd rather not know. Alix Spiegel has our story.
<v Ira Glass>如果你想了解孩子们和被留下的父母对缺席的亲人有多么在意，以及他们到底在想什么，可以看看不孕不育夫妇或单身母亲使用精子库的精子生下孩子时会发生什么。大多数精子库提供各种关于捐赠者的信息。而购买精子的父母实际上可以选择他们想要了解多少。他们希望孩子了解多少。以及他们宁愿不知道多少。阿利克斯·斯皮格尔带来了我们的故事。

00:04:10.650 --> 00:04:53.580
<v Alix Spiegel>The donor is German. His interests include music, literature, and comparative religion. He claims to have scored 780 on his math SATs and says that as a child he excelled at both violin and piano. That in fact, in school he was at the top of his class in music theory. He lists his favorite qualities as discipline, strength, honesty, and order. And when asked what message he would like to convey to the recipients of his semen he writes, "Take the middle path. Avoid extremes. Treat everyone you meet the way that you would like to be treated. And please, read The Razor's Edge by Somerset Maugham."
<v Alix Spiegel>捐赠者是德国人。他的兴趣包括音乐、文学和比较宗教学。他声称他的数学SAT考了780分，并说小时候他在小提琴和钢琴方面都很出色。事实上，他在学校的音乐理论课上名列前茅。他列出自己最喜欢的品质是纪律、力量、诚实和秩序。当被问及想给精子接收者传达什么信息时，他写道：“走中间道路。避免极端。像对待自己一样对待你遇到的每一个人。请阅读萨默塞特·毛姆的《刀锋》。

00:04:53.580 --> 00:05:34.920
<v Alix Spiegel>The donor is Chinese. He says that he is excellent at visualizing abstracts and can, quote, "Take any car apart and learn it inside out." He can run six miles. He can play guitar. His hobbies include microbrews and football. When asked about his ambitions in life he writes that he wants to "help transform the moon for human habitation and create some kind of food supply that will replace meat." He says he's become a sperm donor because he wants to make sure that his genes are perpetuated into the future. He also writes that he's poor. A student. He says he needs the money.
<v Alix Spiegel>捐赠者是华人。他说他擅长抽象视觉化，并引用说：“可以拆解任何汽车并彻底弄懂它。”他能跑六英里。他会弹吉他。他的爱好包括精酿啤酒和足球。当被问及人生的雄心壮志时，他写道他想“帮助改造月球以供人类居住，并创造出一种能替代肉类的食物来源。”他说他成为精子捐赠者是因为他想确保自己的基因能够延续到未来。他还写道他很穷。是个学生。他说他需要这笔钱。

00:05:34.920 --> 00:05:55.670
<v Alix Spiegel>There are six-foot philosophers and pot-smoking surfers, future doctors, out-of-work computer programmers, and hopeful actors who are currently employed at the Dairy Queen. They come in all shapes and sizes, but most of them are there for the money. Young men who walk off the street and are given dirty magazines, a small plastic container, and a private room.
<v Alix Spiegel>这里有六英尺高的哲学家、抽大麻的冲浪者、未来的医生、失业的程序员，还有在冰雪皇后工作的怀揣梦想的演员。他们来自各行各业，高矮胖瘦各不相同，但他们大多数人都是为了钱。这些年轻人走在大街上，被塞给成人杂志、一个小塑料容器和一个私人房间。

00:05:55.670 --> 00:06:28.950
<v Alix Spiegel>But first there are forms to fill out-- health surveys, family histories, essay questions. This information-- and there's a lot of it-- is then compiled into catalogs. Glossy pamphlets filled with men sorted by ethnicity or level of education, and then sent through the mail. Sometimes to infertile couples, sometimes to single women. People whose lives haven't worked out quite the way they had hoped, but who are ready to cut their losses, shelve their doubts, and proceed with plan B.
<v Alix Spiegel>但首先需要填写表格——健康调查、家庭病史、问答题。这些信息（数量相当多）随后被汇编成目录。精美的册子，里面按种族或受教育程度对男人进行了分类，然后通过邮寄发送。有时发给不孕不育的夫妇，有时发给单身女性。这些人的人生并没有如他们所愿，但他们准备好减少损失，搁置怀疑，推进B计划。

00:06:28.950 --> 00:06:37.450
<v Karen>This isn't about falling in love with some guy. It's about making a very scientific decision.
<v Karen>这与爱上某个男人无关。这是关于做一个非常科学的决定。

00:06:37.450 --> 00:06:47.220
<v Alix Spiegel>On the first page of my reporter's notebook, under the heading Karen in Brooklyn, there are these three words-- organized, tasteful, and fantasy.
<v Alix Spiegel>在我的记者笔记本的第一页，在“布鲁克林的凯伦”标题下，有这三个词——有条理、有品位、幻想。

00:06:47.220 --> 00:07:12.580
<v Alix Spiegel>The first two words refer to Karen's apartment, a spacious one-bedroom in a fancy apartment house two blocks from the park in Park Slope, Brooklyn. The third word, fantasy, refers to something that Karen said to me in the first five minutes of our interview. She assured me, in the most reasonable tone possible, that the traditional idea of parenthood was a myth, a bill of goods we sold ourselves in order to bolster the illusion of control.
<v Alix Spiegel>前两个词指的是凯伦的公寓，一套宽敞的一居室，位于布鲁克林公园坡公园旁两个街区的一栋豪华公寓楼里。第三个词“幻想”，指的是凯伦在采访开始的前五分钟里对我说的某件事。她用最理性的语气向我保证，传统的父母观念是一个神话，是我们为了支撑控制的错觉而向自己兜售的商品。

00:07:12.580 --> 00:07:46.160
<v Karen>You think because you marry somebody and you love them and you get together and you're going to have a child that you can in some way predict what your kid's going to be like. But you can't. Your kid's going to be something else. Your kid's going to be its own thing. Every parent I've ever spoken to has told me that their kid sort of came out fully formed, just the way that they are. And that the kid just sort of unfolds. So there's no more guarantee that I'm going to be able to identify what my kid's going to be like by picking a donor as there is by picking a husband.
<v Karen>你以为因为你嫁给了某人，你爱他们，你们在一起了，要生个孩子，你就能以某种方式预测你的孩子会是什么样。但你不能。你的孩子会是别的样子。你的孩子会是其自身。我采访过的每位父母都告诉我，他们的孩子生出来时某种程度上就已经完全定型了，就是他们现在的样子。孩子只是在成长。所以，挑选捐赠者能识别出孩子会是什么样，并不比挑选丈夫更有保障。

00:07:46.160 --> 00:08:10.150
<v Alix Spiegel>Armed with this idea, Karen developed a theory. I'll call it the theory of genetic variety. She believed that the health of her child would be substantially improved if she could find a donor who was the genetic opposite of herself. As an Eastern European Jew, she was therefore searching, she told me, for something along the lines of Cuban Swede, or maybe, Siberian Argentinian.
<v Alix Spiegel>带着这个想法，凯伦发展出了一种理论。我称之为基因多样性理论。她认为，如果她能找到一个与自己基因相反的捐赠者，她孩子的健康状况会大大改善。作为一名东欧犹太人，她告诉我，她正在寻找类似“古巴裔瑞典人”或者“西伯利亚裔阿根廷人”的人。

00:08:10.150 --> 00:08:30.750
<v Alix Spiegel>She talked about this theory for a pretty long time, carefully emphasizing that this process was not about love, but about locating what she called "a little genetic material." Then she brought out the folders, a stack of catalogs almost a foot high which Karen had ranked using a system of checks and x's. We begin at the top.
<v Alix Spiegel>她谈论这个理论谈了很长时间，小心翼翼地强调这个过程不是关于爱，而是关于定位她所说的“一点基因材料”。然后她拿出了文件夹，那是厚度近一英尺的目录，凯伦用勾和叉的系统对它们进行了排名。我们从顶部开始。

00:08:30.750 --> 00:08:46.240
<v Karen>Brown eyes, brown straight hair, medium skin tone. And then his education. This guy had a bachelor's in psychology. A lot of them are students, so you're not going to get much-- their occupation is probably what they want to be rather than what they are.
<v Karen>棕色眼睛，直棕发，中等肤色。还有他的教育程度。这家伙有心理学学士学位。他们中很多人是学生，所以你不会得到太多信息——他们的职业可能更反映他们想成为什么，而不是他们现在的身份。

00:08:46.240 --> 00:08:53.110
<v Alix Spiegel>Now that's kind of interesting to me. I mean, did it feel weird to you at all that you would be having a child with a much younger man?
<v Alix Spiegel>这对我来说很有趣。我是说，你会和一个年轻得多的男人生孩子，这感觉奇怪吗？

00:08:53.110 --> 00:08:55.460
<v Karen>Oh my god, it's so bizarre.
<v Karen>天哪，这太离奇了。

00:08:55.460 --> 00:09:03.510
<v Alix Spiegel>I should mention here that although Karen looks extremely young, with a small thin frame and smooth tan face, she's actually 39.
<v Alix Spiegel>我应该在这里提一下，虽然凯伦看起来非常年轻，身材瘦小，皮肤光滑黝黑，但她其实已经39岁了。

00:09:03.510 --> 00:09:10.710
<v Karen>When I saw if they felt really young and boyish for whatever reason, I couldn't use them. I had to pass.
<v Karen>当我看到他们无论出于什么原因感觉真的很年轻、很孩子气时，我就不能用他们。我必须放弃。

00:09:10.710 --> 00:09:12.200
<v Alix Spiegel>Even though it's just genetic material?
<v Alix Spiegel>即使这只是基因材料？

00:09:12.200 --> 00:09:36.780
<v Karen>Absolutely. I couldn't control-- I didn't predict that seeing the age would send this really weird feeling through me, but it just did. And I went to my email group about this and a lot of other women shared this same feeling, that it just feels funny. I want to almost say incestuous. I want to almost say-- what's the word? Not incestuous, but there's a word for that when you--
<v Karen>绝对。我无法控制——我没料到看到年龄会给我带来这种非常奇怪的感觉，但它确实发生了。我去我的电子邮件群里讨论了这个，很多其他女性分享了同样的感受，觉得这感觉很有意思。我几乎想说乱伦。我几乎想说——那个词是什么？不是乱伦，但当你……时有一个词。

00:09:36.780 --> 00:09:37.130
<v Alix Spiegel>Illegal?
<v Alix Spiegel>违法的？

00:09:37.130 --> 00:09:41.010
<v Karen>Yeah, exactly. It's like, statutory rape.
<v Karen>对，没错。就像是法定强奸。

00:09:41.010 --> 00:09:53.260
<v Alix Spiegel>It was a sentiment which didn't square very well with the notion of scientific process. After all, the sperm of an 18-year-old is no more or less mature than the sperm of an 88-year-old, as Karen herself admits.
<v Alix Spiegel>这种情绪与科学过程的概念并不太吻合。毕竟，正如凯伦自己承认的那样，18岁男性的精子与88岁男性的精子并没有什么成熟度上的差别。

00:09:53.260 --> 00:10:17.930
<v Karen>You know, even though this is a process that I have tried to make very sort of organized and scientific, there's this part of you that you can't really control and you can't really predict. And it just leaps out and you're like, I don't like this guy. I don't know why.
<v Karen>你知道，尽管这是一个我努力使之变得非常有条理和科学的过程，但你内心深处有部分是你无法真正控制和预测的。它突然冒出来，你会觉得，我不喜欢这家伙。我不知道为什么。

00:10:17.930 --> 00:10:35.660
<v Alix Spiegel>That part of Karen, which she could not really control or predict, kept intruding on her search, no matter what her best intentions were. There was the problem of the red-headed donor, for example, a man who was clearly an inappropriate choice, but whose red hair attracted Karen because it reminded her of a college boyfriend.
<v Alix Spiegel>凯伦无法真正控制或预测的那部分自我，无论她意愿多么好，总是在她的搜寻过程中不断干扰。比如那个红头发捐赠者的问题，这个男人显然是个不合适的选择，但他的红头发吸引了凯伦，因为这让她想起了大学时的男朋友。

00:10:35.660 --> 00:10:51.580
<v Alix Spiegel>Then one day, Karen stumbled on another donor. His test scores weren't as high as some others. His family had some health problems which gave Karen pause. Still, there was something in his essay answers which caught Karen's eye, a quality of speech that she found somehow familiar.
<v Alix Spiegel>然后有一天，凯伦偶然发现了另一个捐赠者。他的考试成绩不如其他人高。他的家庭有一些健康问题，这让凯伦犹豫了。尽管如此，他在问答题中的回答中有些东西引起了凯伦的注意，一种她觉得以某种方式很熟悉的说话质感。

00:10:51.580 --> 00:11:17.290
<v Karen>You know, and he named all these nuanced ways of experiencing the world. I just thought, oh, wow. This guy's complicated in a way that I'm complicated and that I appreciate completely in other people. He just felt completely right. And he was my guy. That was the guy.
<v Karen>你知道，他命名了所有这些体验世界的细微差别。我当时就想，哦，哇。这家伙的复杂方式和我一样，而且我在其他人身上非常欣赏这种复杂。他感觉完全对了。他就是我的那个人。就是他。

00:11:17.290 --> 00:11:26.030
<v Alix Spiegel>After her decision, Karen found herself thinking about her donor more and more. She read over his information packet and talked about him frequently with friends.
<v Alix Spiegel>做出决定后，凯伦发现自己越来越多地想到她的捐赠者。她反复阅读他的信息包，并经常和朋友谈论他。

00:11:26.030 --> 00:11:51.300
<v Karen>You know, I knew what the sister did and that she had a kid. And I started thinking, oh, this is cool. Look at all the languages they speak. And I played violin and he plays violin. And I felt like it was good and right and positive. And I just thought it was going to work out well, that I was bringing good stuff to a kid.
<v Karen>你知道，我知道他姐姐做什么，而且她有孩子。我开始想，哦，这很酷。看看他们说的所有语言。我拉过小提琴，他也拉小提琴。我觉得这很好、很正确且积极。我只是觉得这会成功，我在给孩子带来好的东西。

00:11:51.300 --> 00:12:19.610
<v Karen>And then I called up one day to order. I used to order in batches, like a couple-- not just one at a time, but I was trying to psych myself out by over-ordering. You know, because I figured I'd rather get stuck with extra sperm than no kid. So let's do it that way. And I just called up, after I'd gotten my period yet again. And I called up to order some more. They were like, oh, it's not available. And I was like, what?
<v Karen>然后有一天我打电话订购。我过去常按批次订购，比如几个——不是一次一个，我试图通过超额订购来让自己安心。因为我想，我宁愿多存点精子也不愿没孩子。所以就这样做吧。在我又一次月经后，我打电话过去订购更多。他们说，哦，已经没货了。我说，什么？

00:12:19.610 --> 00:12:40.510
<v Karen>Now, everybody will tell you be prepared for this. I was totally unprepared. I had gotten so attached to this donor that I think I cried. I think I just sat down and cried.
<v Karen>现在，每个人都会告诉你对此要有心理准备。我完全没准备好。我对这个捐赠者产生了如此深的依恋，我想我哭了。我想我只是坐下来哭了起来。

00:12:40.510 --> 00:13:12.140
<v Alix Spiegel>Without meaning to, without even being fully aware of her own behavior, Karen had gotten attached and transformed her donor into a kind of phantom boyfriend. This isn't uncommon. Several of the women I spoke to described a similar attachment to their first donors. And like the others, after her first pick evaporated, Karen felt a profound sense of loss. She cursed. She cried. Then Karen got out her catalogs and looked again. But this time she didn't dawdle over essay questions. Karen did it by the numbers.
<v Alix Spiegel>无意之中，甚至没有完全意识到自己的行为，凯伦产生了依恋，并将她的捐赠者变成了一种幻影男友。这并不罕见。我采访过的几位女性都描述了对自己第一位捐赠者的类似依恋。而且像其他人一样，在她的第一人选消失后，凯伦感到了一种深深的失落。她咒骂。她哭泣。然后凯伦拿出她的目录重新查看。但这一次，她没有在问答题上磨蹭。凯伦完全按指标办事。

00:13:12.140 --> 00:13:24.750
<v Karen>My second donor, he's a much more elite guy. Because that stuff is just easy to jump on. This guy's got a doctorate. He speaks like four languages. He's obviously brilliant.
<v Karen>我的第二个捐赠者，他是一个更精英的人。因为那些东西很容易判断。这家伙有博士学位。他会说四种语言。他显然很聪明。

00:13:24.750 --> 00:13:27.680
<v Alix Spiegel>Do you feel less emotionally attached to this second donor?
<v Alix Spiegel>你对这第二个捐赠者的情感依恋会少一些吗？

00:13:27.680 --> 00:13:30.550
<v Karen>I do. I do.
<v Karen>确实少了。是的。

00:13:30.550 --> 00:13:47.670
<v Alix Spiegel>And this created its own problem. Karen wanted some kind of connection, something which would allow her to feel that the father of her child was not simply a random stranger in a sperm bank. Which is actually what he was. But a worthy man, someone to respect and be proud of.
<v Alix Spiegel>这就产生了它自己的问题。凯伦想要某种联系，一种让她觉得她孩子的父亲不仅仅是精子库里的随机陌生人——这实际上就是他的身份——而是一个值得尊敬和骄傲的有价值的男人。

00:13:47.670 --> 00:14:14.760
<v Alix Spiegel>Put another way, Karen wanted some story which explained her choice. A story that she could tell to herself. A story that someday, she could tell to her child. It was what she had to offer in lieu of something more substantive and she wanted to get it right. In fact, this need for a connection, for some narrative which justifies the choice, is so profound, so important to this process, that Karen has decided to improvise a relationship with her second donor.
<v Alix Spiegel>换句话说，凯伦想要一个解释她选择的故事。一个她可以告诉自己的故事。一个未来有一天，她可以告诉她孩子的故事。这是她在缺少实质性东西的情况下所能提供的，她想把这件事做好。事实上，这种对联系的需求，对某种能证明选择合理性的叙事的需求，是如此深刻，对这个过程如此重要，以至于凯伦决定即兴创作一段与她第二个捐赠者的关系。

00:14:14.760 --> 00:14:27.430
<v Alix Spiegel>The brilliant doctoral student with high marks in everything is originally from Spain. So Karen is planning a trip, a kind of ghost honeymoon with the idea of a dad, performed for a baby not yet born.
<v Alix Spiegel>那个各科成绩都很高的聪明博士生最初来自西班牙。所以凯伦正在计划一次旅行，一种鬼魂式的蜜月，怀揣着一个父亲的构想，为还未出生的宝宝进行。

00:14:27.430 --> 00:14:45.150
<v Karen>If I get pregnant with this donor, I will probably go to Barcelona and go to that region. Just so that I can have done that while I was pregnant with this child whose father probably came from that region. Just so I could have that to share with this kid.
<v Karen>如果我怀上了这个捐赠者的孩子，我可能会去巴塞罗那和那个地区。就为了能在怀着这个父亲很可能来自那个地区的孩子时完成这件事。就为了能和这个孩子分享这些。

00:14:45.150 --> 00:14:48.900
<v Alix Spiegel>And that'll be the way that you communicate his father to him?
<v Alix Spiegel>那就是你向他传达他父亲的方式？

00:14:48.900 --> 00:15:16.470
<v Karen>And I'll take a lot of pictures and I'll say, I don't know where-- but you know, this is where they're from. These are your people. And I'll go back with the kid. As soon as the kid's ready to travel, we'll go there. We'll go there and I'll say, this is where your family's from. Part of your family's from here.
<v Karen>我会拍很多照片，我会说，我不知道在哪里——但你知道，这是他们来自的地方。这些人是你的族人。孩子准备好旅行后，我会带着孩子回去。我们会去那里，我会说，这是你家人的来源。你的一部分家人来自这里。

00:15:16.470 --> 00:15:33.940
<v Alix Spiegel>Increase your knowledge and you increase your grief is a Latin proverb I read once, and remember, I guess, because it seemed like a self-defeating way to open a book. But anyway, this is a lesson which Karen, and many of the other women I spoke to, seemed to understand intuitively as they went about choosing their donors.
<v Alix Spiegel>增加知识即增加悲伤，这是我读过的一句拉丁谚语，我之所以记得，我想是因为这似乎是一种自我挫败的开书方式。但无论如何，这似乎是凯伦和我采访过的许多其他女性在选择捐赠者时直觉上就能理解的教训。

00:15:33.940 --> 00:15:57.930
<v Alix Spiegel>In addition to medical histories and essay questions, many of the sperm banks offer pictures, audiotapes, even videos of potential donors. But the women I spoke to didn't seem very interested in these options. Not one of the four wanted to see a picture. They didn't want to watch video interviews either. And only two had chosen to listen to audiotapes. At first this confused me. Then I talked to Jamie.
<v Alix Spiegel>除了病史和问答题，许多精子库还提供照片、录音带，甚至是潜在捐赠者的录像。但我采访的女性似乎对这些选择不太感兴趣。四个人中没有一个想看照片。她们也不想看视频采访。只有两个人选择听录音带。起初这让我很困惑。然后我和杰米谈了谈。

00:15:57.930 --> 00:16:15.570
<v Jamie>I had no idea what I was going to go for. So I said, well, let me go-- if I could pick my husband, what he looks like, and if he was smart or not, that's what I'm going to kind of pick. So you know, tall, dark, handsome, Italian stud sounded good to me.
<v Jamie>我根本不知道我要找什么样的。所以我想，好吧，如果我可以挑选我的丈夫，他长什么样，他是否聪明，这就是我打算挑选的。所以你知道，高大、英俊、意大利种马听起来对我来说不错。

00:16:15.570 --> 00:16:28.480
<v Alix Spiegel>Jamie is a hairdresser who lives in a comfortable old farmhouse in rural upstate New York. When I drive to her home, around 8:00 on a Tuesday night, there are so few lights in the area that it's sometimes difficult to see the road.
<v Alix Spiegel>杰米是一位美发师，住在美国农村的一栋舒适的老农舍里。周二晚上8点左右我开车去她家时，该地区几乎没有灯光，有时很难看清路。

00:16:28.480 --> 00:16:45.930
<v Alix Spiegel>Sitting at her kitchen table, Jamie tells me that even though she's always been independent-- she started her own business in her early 20s-- she somehow knew that she wanted a family. It wasn't until her early 30s, though, when her best friend became pregnant with her third child, that Jamie decided that it was time to start a family of her own.
<v Alix Spiegel>坐在厨房桌边，杰米告诉我，尽管她一直都很独立——她20出头就开始了自己的生意——但她不知何故知道自己想要一个家庭。不过，直到30岁出头，当她最好的朋友怀上第三个孩子时，杰米才决定是时候组建自己的家庭了。

00:16:45.930 --> 00:16:59.010
<v Alix Spiegel>She contacted a sperm bank and immediately found three men that seemed attractive. One donor in particular had caught Jamie's eye. And when the informational packets arrived, it was his tape that Jamie listened to first.
<v Alix Spiegel>她联系了一家精子库，并很快找到了三个看起来很有吸引力的男人。其中一位捐赠者特别吸引了杰米的注意。当信息包寄到时，杰米第一个听的就是他的带子。

00:16:59.010 --> 00:17:17.780
<v Jamie>I listened to his and I was like, oh no. He just didn't sound like anybody that I would ever want to talk with. I don't know, he was just-- I don't know. It's hard to explain. It's just something that I couldn't connect with. I couldn't feel like, oh, he sounds really cool. Not at all. Not at all.
<v Jamie>我听了他的带子，我就想，噢不。他听起来根本不像我想交谈的那种人。我不知道，他就是……我不知道。很难解释。就是某种我无法连接的感觉。我无法觉得，噢，他听起来真的很酷。一点也不。一点也不。

00:17:17.780 --> 00:17:32.430
<v Jamie>And then I said to myself, maybe the audiotapes aren't really a good idea. Maybe it's too much. Because maybe I'm really going to love them on paper and maybe it's going to totally change my outlook.
<v Jamie>然后我对自己说，也许录音带并不是个好主意。也许这太过了。因为也许我在纸面上真的很喜欢他们，也许这会完全改变我的看法。

00:17:32.430 --> 00:17:37.540
<v Alix Spiegel>In that experience of kind of liking someone on paper and then listening to their audiotape, was that like--
<v Alix Spiegel>在那种在纸面上喜欢某人然后听他们录音带的经历中，那是……

00:17:37.540 --> 00:17:53.930
<v Jamie>That was weird. That was definitely weird. That was weird. Because you know, like once you hear someone's voice it makes them real.
<v Jamie>那很奇怪。那绝对很奇怪。那很怪。因为你知道，一旦你听到某人的声音，就让他们变得真实了。

00:17:53.930 --> 00:18:11.540
<v Alix Spiegel>If you ask Jamie to describe her donor, a man she's never seen, she'll tell you that she think he's about six feet tall. She'll say that he has curly brown hair and horn-rimmed glasses. And that when she thinks of him, she imagines him in a tomato garden, standing, laughing, his arm around his grandfather.
<v Alix Spiegel>如果你让杰米描述她的捐赠者，一个她从未见过面的男人，她会告诉你，她觉得他身高大约六英尺。她会说他有一头卷曲的棕发和黑框眼镜。而且当她想到他时，她想象他在番茄园里，站着，笑着，胳膊搭在他祖父的肩上。

00:18:11.540 --> 00:18:32.090
<v Alix Spiegel>If you ask her, really push her, she'll also admit that she sometimes fantasizes that she meets this man and that they fall in love and get married, and live happily ever after with their child. But two seconds later, Jamie will tell you that she knows how ridiculous this sounds. That she doesn't take it seriously. That it's just a thought she had, one that doesn't mean anything.
<v Alix Spiegel>如果你问她，真的逼问她，她也会承认她有时会幻想自己遇见了这个男人，他们坠入爱河并结婚，从此和他们的孩子过上幸福的生活。但两秒钟后，杰米会告诉你她知道这听起来有多荒唐。她并不认真对待这件事。这只是她脑子里闪过的一个念头，并不意味着什么。

00:18:32.090 --> 00:18:49.530
<v Alix Spiegel>Jamie is 32 years old. She allows herself this fantasy and should allow herself this fantasy because she know she's in no danger of mistaking it for a possible reality. But children are different. They're prone to hope and frequent victims of their own imagination.
<v Alix Spiegel>杰米32岁。她允许自己有这种幻想，也应该允许，因为她知道她没有把这误认为现实的可能性。但孩子们不同。他们容易产生希望，也经常是自己想象力的受害者。

00:18:49.530 --> 00:19:26.020
<v Jamie>You know, I don't want them to have a picture in their head of somebody who didn't want to be with them. I want it to just be, OK, it was a man who let us borrow these special seeds. That it was very generous of him. But I don't want to tell the child, well, this is what I thought he looked like and this is where I think he lived. I don't want to paint that whole big fantasy picture for a kid, and then that person's not there.
<v Jamie>你知道，我不希望他们脑子里有一个不想和他们在一起的人的形象。我希望这只是，好的，有一个男人借给了我们这些特别的种子。他非常慷慨。但我不想告诉孩子，嗯，这就是我想象中他长得样子，这就是我觉得他住的地方。我不希望为孩子描绘出那种宏大的幻想图景，而那个人却不在那里。

00:19:26.020 --> 00:19:57.770
<v Alix Spiegel>This is a situation where less is better. Better that the child not think too carefully about who or what his father is. Better that the women avoid pictures, avoid video. In fact, I would make a further argument, that perversely, even though creating a child is one of the most intimate things two people can do, this entire process depends on anonymity, on all parties remaining as abstract an abstraction as possible. To understand why this might be, consider the issue of the yes donor.
<v Alix Spiegel>这是一种少即是多的情况。孩子最好不要太仔细地思考谁或什么是他的父亲。女性最好避免看照片，避免看视频。事实上，我还要进一步论证，奇怪的是，尽管创造孩子是两个人能做的最私密的事情之一，但整个过程依赖于匿名，依赖于所有各方尽可能保持抽象的抽象感。要理解为什么会这样，考虑一下“肯定型捐赠者”的问题。

00:19:57.770 --> 00:20:08.540
<v Alix Spiegel>A yes donor is sperm bank speak for a man who is willing to be contacted after the child turns 18. In other words, a potential real father that the kid could look forward to meeting.
<v Alix Spiegel>“肯定型捐赠者”是精子库术语，指愿意在孩子年满18岁后被联系的男人。换句话说，是一个孩子可以期待见到的潜在真实父亲。

00:20:08.540 --> 00:20:25.090
<v Alix Spiegel>On the surface, the yes donor option seems like a good idea. It certainly seems like a better deal than never meeting your father at all. But when I ask Karen if she's ever considered using a yes donor, she looks down in her lap for a second and sighs a little before answering. Then she shakes her head no.
<v Alix Spiegel>表面上看，肯定型捐赠者的选择听起来是个好主意。它看起来当然比永远见不到父亲要划算。但当我问凯伦是否考虑过使用肯定型捐赠者时，她低头看着自己的腿，叹了口气，然后才回答。接着她摇了摇头，表示拒绝。

00:20:25.090 --> 00:20:58.490
<v Karen>When I made the decision to be a single mother, part of the challenge, part of what I knew I was giving to this kid that was something I was going to have to think deeply and confront again and again, was that I was bringing a kid into the world who is not going to know their father. Period. So the idea of someone who the kid could later contact, that wasn't appealing. Because the message that I'm going to have for this kid is, this is our family. Our family consists of me and you and our village. You know, the people around us who love us.
<v Karen>当我决定做单身母亲时，部分挑战，我意识到我给这个孩子的部分东西是我必须深入思考并不断面对的，那就是我带了一个永远不会知道自己父亲的孩子来到这个世界。句号。所以关于一个孩子以后可以联系某人的想法，那并不吸引人。因为我传达给这个孩子的信息是，这是我们的家庭。我们的家庭由我和你，还有我们的村庄组成。你知道，周围那些爱我们的人。

00:20:58.490 --> 00:21:30.480
<v Karen>There's somebody who's your father. We'll never know who he is. He made a decision to do something for a woman like me and we're really grateful about that, but he's not your dad. And maybe that's something to grieve, something to be angry at me about. But I had a vision of having enormous love to offer you and I knew that we could get through this. And that was always what I thought I would bring to being a single mom.
<v Karen>有一个人是你的父亲。我们永远不会知道他是谁。他做出了为像我这样的女性做点什么的决定，我们对此非常感激，但他不是你的爸爸。也许这是值得哀伤、值得对我生气的事情。但我有一个愿景，就是能为你提供巨大的爱，而且我知道我们能熬过这一关。这就是我一直认为自己能带给单身母亲这个身份的东西。

00:21:30.480 --> 00:22:10.660
<v Karen>So being able to hold out the promise to the kid that they can meet this person, it wasn't part of that. I couldn't imagine raising a kid to say, well, one day you'll meet your dad. Well, what if one day he meets his dad and he sucks? Or he's changed his mind and he's rejectful. I don't want to raise a kid with a fantasy where it's going to be blown to bits. The truth of the matter is, you know what? You don't have a dad. You have a lot of other things.
<v Karen>所以能够对孩子抱有可以见到这个人的承诺，那不是其中的一部分。我无法想象抚养一个孩子去说，嗯，总有一天你会见到你的爸爸。那么，如果有一天他真的见到他爸爸而发现那人是个渣男怎么办？或者他改变了主意，拒绝承认怎么办？我不希望在幻想中抚养一个孩子，然后让那幻想被击得粉碎。事实的真相是，你知道吗？你没有爸爸。你有很多其他的东西。

00:22:10.660 --> 00:22:37.330
<v Alix Spiegel>I know from my conversations with other single moms by choice that the relationship between a mother and her donor changes over time. And that even women resolutely indifferent to their donors during the selection process tend to soften after a child is born. On the message boards, the new mothers talk of the quote "mysterious connection" they suddenly feel to their donors, and write things like, "If I could only send him pictures," or "I wish that he could see."
<v Alix Spiegel>从我与其他选择做单身母亲的女性的谈话中，我知道母亲和捐赠者之间的关系会随着时间推移而改变。而且即使是在选择过程中对捐赠者漠不关心的女性，在孩子出生后也往往会心软。在留言板上，新妈妈们谈论她们突然感觉到的所谓的“神秘联系”，并写下诸如“如果我只能寄照片给他”或者“我希望他能看到”之类的话。

00:22:37.330 --> 00:23:02.860
<v Alix Spiegel>Meanwhile the men, the financially strapped college sophomores, the hopeful actors, the future doctors, continue their lives more or less oblivious. They finish college and go on to graduate programs. They move from LA and switch to real estate. They become actual doctors and start their own practice without realizing that there are three, four, maybe five middle-aged women out there yearning to send them Polaroids of little Johnny's first tooth.
<v Alix Spiegel>与此同时，那些男人们，那些经济拮据的大二学生、怀揣梦想的演员、未来的医生们，继续过着他们的生活，或多或少地浑然不觉。他们大学毕业，进入研究生课程。他们搬离洛杉矶，转行做房地产。他们成为真正的医生，开办自己的诊所，却完全没意识到外面有三、四个，甚至五个中年女性渴望着把小强尼的第一颗牙的照片发给他们。

00:23:02.860 --> 00:23:46.970
<v Alix Spiegel>After talking to a number of these women, I think about that sometimes. I think about lining up all the middle-aged women and all the 20-year-old men and letting them get a good look at one another. I think about the kids and the mean tricks an imagination can play when there's no reality, only absence. And then I think about Karen and I decide that she's right. That if I were a single mom I would say the same thing. You don't have a dad. A father, but not a dad. Your dad doesn't exist. But sit and try to remember, my dear, you have a lot of other things.
<v Alix Spiegel>在和这些女性谈话后，我有时会想到这一点。我想到让所有中年女性和所有20岁的年轻男人排成一排，让他们好好看看对方。我想到孩子们，以及当现实缺失、只有缺席时想象力会玩的那些恶作剧。然后我想到凯伦，决定她是正确的。如果我是单身妈妈，我也会说同样的话。你没有爸爸。有一个生父，但没有爸爸。你的爸爸不存在。但坐下来试着记住，亲爱的，你有很多其他东西。

00:23:46.970 --> 00:23:50.700
<v Ira Glass>Alix Spiegel in New York.
<v Ira Glass>阿利克斯·斯皮格尔，报道于纽约。

00:23:50.700 --> 00:24:11.220
<v Ira Glass>[MUSIC - "LIFE WITHOUT  A FATHER" BY TRAVOLTA W., KEVIN G., AND KEITH L.]
<v Ira Glass>[音乐 - 特拉沃尔塔·W、凯文·G和基思·L表演的《没有父亲的生活》]

00:24:11.220 --> 00:24:13.980
<v Ira Glass>Case Two, Tell It to the Void.
<v Ira Glass>案例二，告诉虚空。

00:24:13.980 --> 00:24:26.400
<v Ira Glass>As Alix Spiegel pointed out, at some point, some moms get a very pointed desire to send photos and information about their kids to forever absent dads. But exactly what information do they want the dads to know? And why, really, do they care if they know it?
