00:35:22.810 --> 00:36:11.030
<v Alexa Junge>Dear Mike, more really big news. Last night O.'s school had its sports banquet and awards evening, which included dinner and dancing. And O. got three trophies, which were the Coach's Award for junior boys basketball, which basically means hardest working and most versatile. He's a wing like Vince Carter and Kobe Bryant are wings. MVP for junior boys volleyball. And get this, Male Athlete of the Year for grade eight. I can't even explain how wrong it is that you don't know this stuff. Unless, of course, you're reading this letter. Which you probably aren't. Which makes me wonder why I bother with this exercise. That's right, tell the void how much your son rocks.
<v Alexa Junge>亲爱的迈克，还有真正的大新闻。昨晚O的学校举行了体育宴会和颁奖之夜，包括晚餐和跳舞。O得了三个奖杯，分别是初中男生篮球教练奖，这基本意味着最勤奋和最多面手。他是侧翼球员，像文斯·卡特和科比·布莱恩特那样。初中男生排球最有价值球员。还有，注意听，八年级年度最佳男运动员。我甚至无法解释你不知道这些东西有多荒谬。除非，当然，你在读这封信。而你可能没在读。这让我怀疑我为什么要做这个练习。没错，告诉虚空你的儿子有多棒。

00:36:11.030 --> 00:36:46.310
<v Alexa Junge>C. Told him how proud he was of him and that he didn't have to do sports if he didn't want to, if he wasn't having fun. And that if he quit all sports today, we'd still love him and still be proud of him. And C. told him how great it was that he was controlling his temper in the games when he lost, or after a bad call, or whatever. And O. said, "I'm just storing it up." And C. Said, "Oh, what are you going to do, explode on the court someday?" And O. said, "Oh, no. Way worse than that." And C. said, "Spit in the ump's face?" And O. said, "Oh, no. Way worse than that."
<v Alexa Junge>C告诉他他为他感到多骄傲，如果他不想参加体育活动也没关系，如果他觉得不开心的话。如果他今天退出所有运动，我们依然爱他，依然为他感到骄傲。C还告诉他，他在比赛中输球、或判罚不公时能控制情绪真是太棒了。O说：“我只是把它存起来。”C说：“哦，你是打算有一天在球场上爆发吗？”O说：“哦，不。比那严重多了。”C说：“吐裁判口水？”O说：“哦，不。比那严重多了。”

00:36:46.310 --> 00:37:29.620
<v Alexa Junge>OK, he was joking, but it reminded me of when you said you used to lose your temper all the time. And then around 12 or 13, after throwing a plate or a bottle of ketchup at your older sister who had taken your Ski-Doo suit or something, you just went, "OK, that's it. I'm not going to get mad anymore. It doesn't help me. It makes it worse. I get in trouble. I look like an idiot and it's stupid." And then, sure enough, I'm trying to think of one time you got mad and I can't. You never got mad. Even when I was such a jerk and stuff was happening. You just put on music and cooked meat and smoked cigarettes and what?
<v Alexa Junge>好吧，他在开玩笑，但这让我想起你说你过去总是发脾气。然后在12或13岁左右，在你朝拿了你滑雪服或什么的姐姐扔盘子或番茄酱瓶后，你只是说：“好吧，就这样。我再也不发火了。这对我没帮助。它只会让情况更糟。我惹麻烦。我看起来像个白痴，这太蠢了。”然后，果然，我试着想一件你生气的事，却想不出来。你从不生气。即使当我是个混蛋，事情在发生时。你只是放音乐，做肉吃，抽香烟，然后呢？

00:37:29.620 --> 00:37:54.580
<v Alexa Junge>Remember when I freaked out and drove myself to the hospital to have O. Because I thought you were too relaxed about the whole thing? And you ran all the way, all those miles of city blocks to the hospital and came in all sweating and red and purple. And I was already dilated and the nurse said you looked like John Lennon. And you said, "I'm here. I'm here." And I said some asshole thing and you said, "Come on. Look what's happening here. This is big. Don't be mad now."
<v Alexa Junge>还记得我发疯，自己开车去医院生O吗？因为我觉得你对整件事太放松了。你一路跑来，跑过了好几个街区的城市街道赶到医院，进来时汗流浃背，满脸通红发紫。我那时已经开宫口了，护士说你看起来像约翰·列侬。你说：“我来了。我来了。”我说了句混蛋话，你说：“快点。看这里发生了什么。这很重大。现在别生气。”

00:37:54.580 --> 00:38:09.460
<v Alexa Junge>All right, that made a lot of sense. But Mike, it's still big. He's still here, still happening. So don't you be mad now.
<v Alexa Junge>好吧，那很有道理。但迈克，它依然重大。他还在这里，依然在发生。所以你现在别生气。

00:38:09.460 --> 00:38:29.400
<v Alexa Junge>See, I'm trying to learn more about O. by remembering what I can of you. It's a little uncanny sometimes how some things are really similar, like this anger thing. Just out of the blue the kid stopped losing his temper at the same age you decided to stop losing your temper. So where does it go when you stop losing it?
<v Alexa Junge>你看，我试图通过回忆我所能记住的关于你的事来了解O。有时有些事情真的非常相似，这有点不可思议，比如这发火的事情。孩子突然在和你决定不再发火的相同年龄停止了发火。所以当你停止发火时，那气去哪了？

00:38:29.400 --> 00:38:55.750
<v Alexa Junge>It would be so cool if you could get back to me, really. I mean, not me. Him. Or me first. Or whatever. Not for some romantic thing. Don't get the wrong idea. C. and me are great, and you're probably married with other athletic kids. Sometimes I wonder if you just decided that it would be best if you slipped away forever, best for O. and me and that you really, really believed that.
<v Alexa Junge>如果你能回复我会很酷，真的。我是说，不是我。他。或者先是我。或者什么的。不是为了什么浪漫的事。别误会。C和我很好，你可能也结婚了，有其他运动型小孩。有时我怀疑你是否只是决定如果悄悄溜走永远消失是最好的，对O和我觉得最好的，并且你真的真的相信那一点。

00:38:55.750 --> 00:39:14.280
<v Alexa Junge>Remember when I stood on the sidewalk in front of my house with O. on my hip? He was about two years old and I screamed my stupid head off at you while you walked away without saying a word. Maybe you were thinking, "This is nuts. This poor kid. I've got to go." That would've made sense at the time, right?
<v Alexa Junge>还记得我站在屋前的人行道上，怀里抱着O吗？他大约两岁，我冲你尖叫我的蠢话，而你走开了，一言不发。也许你在想，“这太疯了。这可怜的孩子。我必须走。”那在当时说得通，对吧？

00:39:14.280 --> 00:39:30.810
<v Alexa Junge>But you know, I've calmed down since then. Even though I was a lot older than 13 when I figured out that losing my temper wasn't getting me anywhere. X in Winnipeg.
<v Alexa Junge>但你知道，从那时起我已经冷静下来了。尽管我发现发脾气没用的时候已经比13岁大多了。温尼伯的X。

00:39:30.810 --> 00:39:55.390
<v Alexa Junge>Dear Mike, well, summer's almost over and as usual, odd things have been happening. This is the latest. Your dad called here, right out of the blue. Haven't seen or talked to him since O. was an infant and you and I took that road trip when you barfed in the hotel bathtub. And he told us that he lived in Australia now, but he was in town and would really like to see O. if O. would like to see him.
<v Alexa Junge>亲爱的迈克，嗯，夏天快结束了，像往常一样，发生了些奇怪的事。这是最近的一件。你爸爸打电话来，突如其来。自O还是婴儿，你和我那次开车旅行在旅馆浴缸里吐了以后，就再也没见过他或和他说话了。他告诉我们他现在住在澳大利亚，但他在城里，如果O想见他，他真的希望能见见O。

00:39:55.390 --> 00:40:09.700
<v Alexa Junge>O. said no. Not because he didn't want to see him really, but because he just didn't know why or what purpose it would serve. And your dad totally understood and said if O. ever changes his mind, he'd be glad. He'd wait.
<v Alexa Junge>O说了不。不是因为他不想见他，而是因为他只是不知道为什么，也不明白有什么意义。你爸爸完全理解，说如果O有一天改变主意，他会很高兴。他会等。

00:40:09.700 --> 00:40:35.320
<v Alexa Junge>I asked him if you were happy, and he said, yes, very. That you live in Tokyo. That you're a very successful chef, an executive chef, he said. In a hotel with 550 rooms. And that you're married to a wonderful Japanese woman and that you have a beautiful three-year-old daughter. And that you'd probably never leave Tokyo. He said he'd send O. a picture of you and your wife and your daughter. He called her O.'s sister.
<v Alexa Junge>我问他你快乐吗，他说，是的，非常快乐。你住在东京。你是位非常成功的厨师，他说，是行政总厨。在一家拥有550个房间的旅馆里。而且你娶了一位出色的日本妻子，有一个漂亮的3岁女儿。而且你可能永远不会离开东京。他说他会寄给O一张你、你妻子和你女儿的照片。他称她为O的妹妹。

00:40:35.320 --> 00:40:52.530
<v Alexa Junge>He also said you were a lot like him, a wanderer, a bit of a loner, and that you often went off by yourself for stretches of time and didn't talk about it much. But he sounded very proud of you. Very protective. As though he was worried I'd slag you to bits or something.
<v Alexa Junge>他还说你很像他，是个漫游者，有点独来独往，经常独自离开一段时间，不怎么谈论它。但他听起来为你感到非常骄傲。非常有保护欲。就好像他担心我会把你贬得一文不值什么的。

00:40:52.530 --> 00:40:58.620
<v Alexa Junge>And of course, I wouldn't do that. Like O. says, what's the point?
<v Alexa Junge>当然，我不会那样做。就像O说的，有什么意义呢？

00:40:58.620 --> 00:41:16.490
<v Alexa Junge>You're lucky, Mike, to have a father standing up for you. Have you introduced your famous penny wieners to the Japanese? Sayonara, X.
<v Alexa Junge>你很幸运，迈克，有一个为你挺身而出的父亲。你把你的成名作热狗介绍给日本人了吗？再见，X。

00:41:16.490 --> 00:41:30.080
<v Ira Glass>Since posting those letters on the internet, X. has identified herself as Miriam Toews. She has a book coming out in the fall in the United States called Swing Low, A Life. Her letters were read for us by Alexa Junge. Read X.'s other letters at openletters.net.
<v Ira Glass>自将这些信件贴在互联网上后，X已确认自己就是米里亚姆·托伊斯。她秋天在美国有一本新书要出版，叫《下摆，生活》。她的信是由亚历克莎·琼格为我们朗读的。请在openletters.net阅读X的其他信件。

00:41:30.080 --> 00:42:44.950
<v Ira Glass>Coming up, suburban child wants unhappy childhood. Adopting couple tries to reunite biological mom and baby. What is this country coming to? In a minute from Public Radio International when our program continues.
<v Ira Glass>接下来，郊区孩子想要不幸的童年。收养夫妇试图让亲生母亲和婴儿团聚。这个国家到底怎么了？国际公共广播电台节目稍后继续。

00:42:44.950 --> 00:42:57.210
<v Ira Glass>It's This American Life. I'm Ira Glass. Each week on our program, of course, we choose a theme, bring you a variety of different kinds of stories on that theme. Today, Stories from the Missing Parents Bureau. We have arrived at case three.
<v Ira Glass>这是《美国生活》。我是艾拉·格拉斯。当然，我们每周都会选择一个主题，为您带来围绕该主题的各种不同故事。今天，来自“失踪父母调查局”的故事。我们来到了第三个案例。

00:42:57.210 --> 00:43:22.110
<v Ira Glass>Case Three, I'm an Orphan; Don't Tell My Mom. This is a story about wanting parents to be gone from the scene. It begins in the 1980s when Starlee Kine, who was 11 years old, decided, among other things, that she wanted to be a child star. With her parents' generous help, she attended a series of classes run by a man named Kevin McDermott. Kevin was regarded as one of the best child acting coaches in the business. Starlee picks up the story.
<v Ira Glass>案例三，我是孤儿，别告诉妈妈。这是一个关于想要父母消失的故事。它开始于20世纪80年代，当时11岁的斯塔莉·凯恩决定，除其他外，她想成为童星。在父母慷慨的帮助下，她参加了由一个叫凯文·麦克德莫特的人开设的一系列课程。凯文被认为是业内最好的童星教练之一。斯塔莉开始讲这个故事。

00:43:22.110 --> 00:43:45.540
<v Starlee Kine>By far, the strangest acting exercise I've ever heard of or participated in is one we did in his class called character group therapy. We were instructed to make up a character, the only guideline being that they had to be troubled in some way. We were given six weeks to develop. At the end of which, an actual certified psychologist came into the class and psychoanalyzed our characters during a group therapy session.
<v Starlee Kine>到目前为止，我听说过或参与过的最奇怪的表演练习是我们班上一个叫“角色团体治疗”的练习。我们被指示编造一个角色，唯一的准则就是他们必须在某种程度上遇到麻烦。我们有六周的时间来发展这个角色。六周结束时，一位真正的专业心理学家走进班级，在小组治疗会议期间对我们的角色进行心理分析。

00:43:45.540 --> 00:44:17.680
<v Starlee Kine>I chose to be an orphan. My character had been abandoned by her parents as a little child. I don't remember her name, but it was something androgynous, like Sam. I'd lived on the street briefly, but for the most part I'd been tossed from one foster home to another. I was terribly suspicious of adults. There was a family that wanted to adopt me and I secretly hoped they would, but was too tough to admit it. The only one I truly trusted was my dog, who I'd found in a gutter or something when he was just a puppy.
<v Starlee Kine>我选择做一个孤儿。我的角色在小时候被父母遗弃了。我不记得她的名字，但它有点中性，像萨姆。我曾短暂地流落街头，但大部分时间是被从一个寄养家庭扔到另一个。我对成年人极其怀疑。有个家庭想领养我，我暗暗希望他们这么做，但又太坚强不愿承认。我唯一真正信任的是我的狗，它是我在他还是幼犬时在阴沟里或其他什么地方捡到的。

00:44:17.680 --> 00:44:52.510
<v Starlee Kine>To the best of my memory, nearly all my fellow classmates picked orphans as well. They had similar back stories filled with uncannily familiar details. I tracked some of these people down, but they remember even less than I do. Probably because afterwards they actually went on to play these characters professionally. All of my classmates were working actors in some capacity or other. You'd see them as you flipped through the channels, playing comatose children on St. Elsewhere , or Ricky Schroder's first kiss on Silver Spoons . Or the young version of an older, more established actor on a sitcom special flashback episode.
<v Starlee Kine>据我的记忆，我几乎所有的同学也都选择了孤儿。他们有类似的背景故事，充满了令人毛骨悚然的熟悉细节。我追踪了这些人中的一些，但他们记得的甚至比我还少。可能是因为事后他们真的去职业扮演这些角色了。我所有的同学都是某种意义上的职业演员。你会看到他们在电视转台时，扮演《圣伊丽莎白》中昏迷的孩子，或者是《银汤匙》中瑞奇·施罗德的初吻对象。或者是情景喜剧特别回忆剧集中老年、更有名的演员的年轻版本。

00:44:52.510 --> 00:45:18.380
<v Starlee Kine>In my entire aspiring career, I didn't land a single role. No commercials, no pilots, no features. Nothing. It's the kind of record that sticks with a person, permanently labeling them, like alcoholism. I will forever be a failed child star.
<v Starlee Kine>在我整个有抱负的生涯中，我没接到一个角色。没有广告，没有试播集，没有电影。什么都没有。这是一种会跟随人一生的记录，永久性地给他们贴上标签，像酗酒一样。我永远是个失败的童星。

00:45:18.380 --> 00:45:25.240
<v Starlee Kine>Kevin still teaches, his reputation as solid as ever. And I visited his current class to see if they were still pretending to be orphans.
<v Starlee Kine>凯文依然在教学，他的名声依然稳固。我参观了他的现任班级，看看他们是否还在假装自己是孤儿。

00:45:25.240 --> 00:45:29.180
<v Kevin McDermott>OK, Grady, let us see an expression that shows teeth and mouth open.
<v Kevin McDermott>好的，格雷迪，让我们看一个露出牙齿且嘴巴张开的表情。

00:45:29.180 --> 00:45:57.290
<v Starlee Kine>The class is in a different location from when I was a student, but some things are the same-- the metal folding chairs, the special spotlight that Kevin uses to cue scenes. The walls in the waiting room are still plastered with children's head shots, including an autographed photo of Marc Price. Marc Price played the neighbor Skippy on Family Ties. He was in love with Mallory. The picture was up when I was in the class and it was always a source of pride to me, that Skippy and I had the same acting teacher.
<v Starlee Kine>班级位置和我做学生时不同，但有些东西是一样的——金属折叠椅，凯文用来提示场景的特殊聚光灯。候诊室的墙上依然贴满了童星头像，包括一张马克·普莱斯的签名照。马克·普莱斯在《凡人琐事》里扮演邻居斯基皮。他爱着马洛里。那张照片我在班里时就在那里，能和斯基皮拥有同一个表演老师一直是我引以为豪的事。

00:45:57.290 --> 00:46:14.380
<v Starlee Kine>Mid series, a back story was added to Skippy's character. Skippy discovered he was adopted as a baby. I point the picture out to the class and they smile politely. It turns out they've never heard of Family Ties.
<v Starlee Kine>系列剧中期，斯基皮的角色被添加了一个背景故事。斯基皮发现他是个婴儿时被收养的。我指着照片对班上同学说，他们礼貌地微笑着。原来他们从来没听说过《凡人琐事》。

00:46:14.380 --> 00:46:30.640
<v Starlee Kine>Kevin still does character group therapy, but now he plays the therapist himself. It begins the same way it always has. The kids sit in a circle, they do a muscle relaxation exercise, and then Kevin counts them down into it, like he's hypnotizing them.
<v Starlee Kine>凯文依然做角色团体治疗，但现在他自己扮演治疗师。它以一贯的方式开始。孩子们围坐成一圈，做肌肉放松练习，然后凯文倒数让他们进入状态，就像他在催眠他们一样。

00:46:30.640 --> 00:47:02.370
<v Kevin McDermott>Three, you're in the peer group counseling session. Two, you are this character. One, welcome. Some of you are new. Some of you started last week in our first group. I'm Dr. Roberts, for those of you who are new tonight. And I'd like to go around the room and I'd like everyone to tell us their first name and anything about themselves, or why they're here, that they would like to share.
<v Kevin McDermott>三，你在同伴咨询会议中。二，你是这个角色。一，欢迎。你们中有些人是新来的。有些人上周参加了我们第一组。我是罗伯茨博士，为今晚新来的同学介绍一下。我想绕着房间转一圈，让每个人告诉我们他们的名字，以及任何关于他们自己，或者为什么他们在这里，他们想分享的内容。

00:47:02.370 --> 00:47:12.250
<v Starlee Kine>There's a stunning absence of orphans. In their place is a potpourri of trendy WB-style back stories and scandalous hour-long drama side plots.
<v Starlee Kine>孤儿惊人地缺席了。取而代之的是各种流行WB风格的背景故事和可耻的一小时戏剧侧线情节。

00:47:12.250 --> 00:47:41.050
<v Starlee Kine>One boy impregnated his high school girlfriend and is full of despair over the possibility of her getting an abortion. There's a girl in an abusive relationship. Another boy cuts up his arms after his father becomes an alcoholic. One of the characters saw her boyfriend get killed in a drive-by shooting, an idea the actress got from an audition for The Practice she'd gone on right before class. The only kid whose back story is remotely orphan-like is played by a boy named Robert [? Negron. ?]
<v Starlee Kine>一个男孩让他的高中女友怀孕了，对她可能堕胎充满绝望。有一个女孩处于虐待关系中。另一个男孩在父亲变成酒鬼后割自己的胳膊。其中一个角色看到男友在驾车枪击中被杀，这个灵感来自这位女演员在下课前去参加的《律师本色》试镜。背景故事最接近孤儿的一个孩子是由一个叫罗伯特·内格隆的男孩扮演的。

00:47:41.050 --> 00:47:46.690
<v Starlee Kine>He doesn't cry. He doesn't even like talking about his feelings. He could care less about the other kids' problems.
<v Starlee Kine>他不哭。他甚至不喜欢谈论自己的感受。他根本不在乎其他孩子的问题。

00:47:46.690 --> 00:47:50.390
<v Robert>I'm kind of over it to tell you the truth.
<v Robert>说实话，我有点厌倦了。

00:47:50.390 --> 00:47:52.650
<v Kevin McDermott>OK.
<v Kevin McDermott>好的。

00:47:52.650 --> 00:48:10.320
<v Robert>If my car breaks down, I don't form a club with other people whose cars are broken down. I go to someone whose car is running, for god's sakes. Jesus.
<v Robert>如果我的车坏了，我不会和其他车坏了的人组个俱乐部。看在上帝份上，我会找个车能跑的人。天哪。

00:48:10.320 --> 00:48:21.640
<v Starlee Kine>These kids don't understand what being a real fake orphan is all about. Back in the day, orphans were strictly of the Punky Brewster, Webster variety-- sincere, saccharine, cloying.
<v Starlee Kine>这些孩子不明白什么是真正的假孤儿。在过去，孤儿严格来说是《庞奇·布鲁斯特》或《韦伯斯特》风格的——真诚、肉麻、矫情。

00:48:21.640 --> 00:48:42.040
<v Starlee Kine>In my class, just as one kid finished sobbing his eyes out, another would start, and rightly so. Now there seems to be a trend of more realistic, less damp performances. In places of sap, the kids seem to challenge their depression into snappy, teenage angst, opting to come across deep instead of sniveling.
<v Starlee Kine>在我班上，正如一个孩子哭得眼泪汪汪，另一个会开始哭，而且理应如此。现在似乎有一种更现实、没那么潮湿表演的趋势。在矫情的地方，孩子们似乎把他们的抑郁转化为急促的、青少年的焦虑，选择表现得深沉而不是抽泣。

00:48:42.040 --> 00:48:43.790
<v Kevin McDermott>Name.
<v Kevin McDermott>名字。

00:48:43.790 --> 00:48:46.710
<v Jeremy>I'm Jeremy.
<v Jeremy>我是杰里米。

00:48:46.710 --> 00:48:52.510
<v Kevin McDermott>Jeremy, what makes you feel sadness in your life?
<v Kevin McDermott>杰里米，什么让你在生活中感到悲伤？

00:48:52.510 --> 00:49:02.820
<v Jeremy>Sadness. Sadness usually comes from waking up in the morning.
<v Jeremy>悲伤。悲伤通常来自早上醒来。

00:49:02.820 --> 00:49:05.270
<v Kevin McDermott>Why is that a sad time?
<v Kevin McDermott>为什么那是悲伤的时间？

00:49:05.270 --> 00:49:11.760
<v Jeremy>I don't know. I guess the show goes on.
<v Jeremy>不知道。我猜演出还得继续。

00:49:11.760 --> 00:49:15.930
<v Kevin McDermott>What show goes on?
<v Kevin McDermott>什么演出继续？

00:49:15.930 --> 00:49:26.270
<v Jeremy>You know, the show. The show we have to show people.
<v Jeremy>你知道，那个演出。我们必须表演给别人看的演出。

00:49:26.270 --> 00:50:03.460
<v Starlee Kine>By the end of the class it was clear. We all chose to be orphans because at the time, they were the norm, the given. Arnold, Willis, Fonzie, Ponyboy, Annie-- orphans dominated both the big and small screen. They were even recruited to breathe new life into the classics. When a show's ratings would drop, one of two things most likely happened. A baby was born or an orphan was discovered sleeping in the hall or shoplifting from the store. Soon after, the orphan became a permanent member of the cast. We picked orphans in response to market pressures, just like Kevin's students today.
<v Starlee Kine>到班级结束时，这很清楚了。我们当时选择成为孤儿是因为那时他们是常态，是默认设定。阿诺德、威利斯、冯兹、小马哥、安妮——孤儿统治了大银幕和小荧幕。他们甚至被招募来为经典剧集注入活力。当剧集收视率下降时，最可能发生两件事之一。一个婴儿出生了，或者一个孤儿被发现在大厅睡觉或在商店行窃。不久后，这个孤儿就成了常驻角色。我们选择孤儿是对市场压力的回应，就像今天凯文的学生一样。

00:50:03.460 --> 00:50:05.050
<v Kevin McDermott>Name?
<v Kevin McDermott>名字？

00:50:05.050 --> 00:50:06.030
<v Scott>Johnny.
<v Scott>强尼。

00:50:06.030 --> 00:50:06.800
<v Kevin McDermott>Last name?
<v Kevin McDermott>姓氏？

00:50:06.800 --> 00:50:08.190
<v Scott>Calzone.
<v Scott>卡尔佐内。

00:50:08.190 --> 00:50:08.460
<v Kevin McDermott>Age?
<v Kevin McDermott>年龄？

00:50:08.460 --> 00:50:09.240
<v Scott>Want me to spell it for you?
<v Scott>想让我帮你拼出来吗？

00:50:09.240 --> 00:50:12.150
<v Kevin McDermott>No, that's OK. You seem to have kind of an attitude, Johnny.
<v Kevin McDermott>不，没事。你似乎有点态度，强尼。

00:50:12.150 --> 00:50:13.050
<v Scott>Yeah?
<v Scott>是吗？

00:50:13.050 --> 00:50:14.840
<v Kevin McDermott>Yeah.
<v Kevin McDermott>是。

00:50:14.840 --> 00:50:23.280
<v Starlee Kine>That was Scott [? Newson. ?] He tells me he's been watching a lot of The Sopranos lately. But he says the name Johnny Calzone just popped into his head.
<v Starlee Kine>那是斯科特·纽森。他告诉我他最近看了很多《黑道家族》。但他说是名字强尼·卡尔佐内突然出现在他脑海里的。

00:50:23.280 --> 00:50:25.230
<v Kevin McDermott>Tell us a little bit about why you're here.
<v Kevin McDermott>告诉我们一点为什么你在这里。

00:50:25.230 --> 00:50:30.880
<v Scott>Because I [BLEEP] love your group.
<v Scott>因为我[消音]喜欢你的小组。

00:50:30.880 --> 00:50:48.270
<v Starlee Kine>Having said all that, I do think there's a personal element to choosing these characters. For the most part, these kids are from upper-middle-class families that are pretty supportive. Playing these parts, they aren't rebelling against their families as much as fantasizing about what life is like on the other side.
<v Starlee Kine>说了这么多，我确实认为选择这些角色有一个个人因素。在很大程度上，这些孩子来自相当支持他们的中上层家庭。扮演这些角色，他们与其说是在反抗家庭，不如说是在幻想另一边的生活是什么样子的。

00:50:48.270 --> 00:51:11.040
<v Starlee Kine>In my case, I chose to be an orphan partly because I came from the kind of home where if my sister and I wanted to visit our friends who live directly across the street, instead of letting us just walk across, my mom would put us in the car, back the car out of our driveway across a deserted, residential street, and up into their driveway to eliminate all chance of us getting killed in a hit and run.
<v Starlee Kine>就我而言，我选择做一个孤儿部分是因为我来自这样一个家庭，如果我和我妹妹想去拜访住在街道对面的朋友，我妈妈不会让我们直接走过去，而是会把我们放进车里，把车从车道倒出，穿过一条荒无人烟的住宅街道，倒进他们的车道，以消除我们被车撞死的任何机会。

00:51:11.040 --> 00:51:26.940
<v Starlee Kine>The orphan's life, though was all about freedom. Orphans didn't have to answer to anyone. Didn't have a well-intentioned but irrational adult in charge of their every move. They had their smarts, they had their sidekick pet, they had their broken locket, and they had themselves. That was it.
<v Starlee Kine>然而，孤儿的生活完全是关于自由的。孤儿不需要对任何人负责。没有一个出发点很好但不理智的成年人掌管他们的一举一动。他们有自己的聪明才智，他们有自己的跟班宠物，他们有破旧的小挂坠盒，他们有他们自己。就是这样。

00:51:26.940 --> 00:51:40.530
<v Starlee Kine>During my years in Kevin's class, a rope ladder made of tied-together bed sheets permanently hung from my suburban ranch house's second-story window for when I would have to sneak out in the middle of the night in search of my real parents. That was my favorite game.
<v Starlee Kine>在凯文班级的那些年里，我郊区牧场式房子的二楼窗户上永久悬挂着一架用床单绑成的绳梯，那是为了我需要在深夜溜出去寻找亲生父母时用的。那是由于我最喜欢的游戏。

00:51:40.530 --> 00:51:53.800
<v Starlee Kine>Character group therapy was just as good. Being in Kevin's class, it sometimes wasn't clear whether I was aspiring to be a child star orphan or an actual orphan.
<v Starlee Kine>角色团体治疗也一样好。在凯文的班里，有时不清楚我是在渴望成为一个童星孤儿还是一个真正的孤儿。

00:51:53.800 --> 00:51:59.520
<v Ira Glass>Starlee Kine is one of the producers of our program.
<v Ira Glass>斯塔莉·凯恩是我们节目的制片人之一。

00:51:59.520 --> 00:52:02.230
<v Ira Glass>Case Four, Runaway Mom.
<v Ira Glass>案例四，逃跑的妈妈。

00:52:02.230 --> 00:52:15.570
<v Ira Glass>Three years ago, Dan Savage and his boyfriend, Terry, adopted a baby. It was an open adoption, the kind where the birth parents choose the adoptive parents, and then stay in touch after handing over the child. But things have not been working out according to plan.
<v Ira Glass>三年前，丹·萨维奇和他的男友泰瑞收养了一个婴儿。那是一次开放式收养，即亲生父母选择养父母，并在交出孩子后保持联系。但事情并没有按计划进行。

00:52:15.570 --> 00:52:36.610
<v Dan Savage>We decided to go ahead and try for an open adoption because we wanted our kid's biological parents to be a part of his life. We did this even though our agency warned us that we might be in for a long wait. The birth mothers who spoke at a two-day seminar we attended both said that finding good Christian homes for their babies was their first concern. Birth control wasn't a concern for them, apparently.
<v Dan Savage>我们决定尝试进行开放式收养，因为我们希望孩子亲生父母能成为他生命的一部分。尽管我们机构警告我们可能需要漫长的等待，我们还是这么做了。我们参加的两天研讨会上发言的两位亲生母亲都说，为她们的宝宝找到好的基督教家庭是她们的首要考虑。显然避孕并不是她们的考虑。

00:52:36.610 --> 00:53:06.530
<v Dan Savage>As it turns out, we didn't have to wait long for a birth mother to come along who believed being born once was enough. A few weeks after our paperwork was done, we got a call from the agency. A 19-year-old street kid named Melissa, homeless by choice and seven months pregnant by accident, had selected my boyfriend and I from our adoption agency's pool of pre-screened parent wannabes. The day we met Melissa, the agency suggested that all three of us go out for lunch to get to know each other. We were bursting with touchy-feely questions, which we soon realized was a problem.
<v Dan Savage>事实证明，我们没等多久就有一位认为生一次孩子就足够了的亲生母亲出现了。我们的文书工作完成后几周，我们接到了机构的电话。一个名叫梅丽莎的19岁街头少年，选择流浪且意外怀孕七个月，从我们的收养机构筛选出的候补父母库中选中了我和我男友。我们见到梅丽莎的那天，机构建议我们三个人一起出去吃午餐互相了解一下。我们充满了感性十足的问题，很快意识到这是个问题。

00:53:06.530 --> 00:53:22.640
<v Dan Savage>Stoic and wary, Melissa was only interested in the facts. She was pregnant. She couldn't have a baby on the streets. And so she was doing an adoption.
<v Dan Savage>坚忍且警惕的梅丽莎只对事实感兴趣。她怀孕了。她没法在街头抚养孩子。所以她决定收养。

00:53:22.640 --> 00:53:28.970
<v Dan Savage>We were with Melissa when DJ was born and we were in her hospital room two days later when it was time for her to give him up.
<v Dan Savage>DJ出生时我们就在现场，两天后当她要把他交出来时，我们也在她的病房里。

00:53:28.970 --> 00:53:48.990
<v Dan Savage>Before we could take DJ home, we had to take him out of his mother's arms as she sat sobbing in her bed. It was the hardest thing I've ever done in my life. I was 33 years old when we adopted DJ and I thought I knew what a broken heart was. I thought mine had been broken a couple of times. I thought I knew how a broken heart felt and what it looked like. I didn't know anything.
<v Dan Savage>在我们带DJ回家之前，我们必须把他从坐在床上啜泣的母亲怀里抱走。那是我一生中做过的最艰难的事情。我们收养DJ时我33岁，我以为我了解什么是心碎。我以为我的心曾被伤过几次。我以为我知道心碎的感觉和样子。我什么都不知道。

00:53:48.990 --> 00:54:15.180
<v Dan Savage>You know what a broken heart looks like? Like a sobbing teenager in a hospital bed giving a two-day-old infant she knows she can't take care of to a couple she hopes can. There was nothing remotely uplifting about the moment. She didn't smile bravely through her tears and say, take good care of my baby. She folded up and sobbed and we stood there, feeling like monsters.
<v Dan Savage>你知道心碎是什么样子的吗？就像一个啜泣的青少年在病床上，把自己明知无法照顾的出生两天的婴儿交给一对她希望可以照顾好的夫妇。那一刻没有丝毫令人振奋的地方。她没能在泪水中勇敢微笑说，请好好照顾我的宝宝。她崩溃啜泣，我们站在那里，感觉像怪物。

00:54:15.180 --> 00:54:29.300
<v Dan Savage>We didn't meet Bacchus, our son's birth father, until almost a year after we'd adopted DJ. He was homeless, too, and Bacchus was his street name. Melissa hooked up with him for a few weeks one summer and by the time she realized she was pregnant, the God of Wine was gone.
<v Dan Savage>我们在收养DJ几乎一年后才见到巴克斯，我们儿子的亲生父亲。他也是流浪者，巴克斯是他的街头名字。梅丽莎那个夏天和他在一起了几周，当她意识到怀孕时，这位酒神已经离开了。

00:54:29.300 --> 00:54:55.750
<v Dan Savage>When we adopted DJ, Bacchus didn't know he was a father, or that his son had been adopted by a gay couple. So we were tense when Bacchus surfaced in New Orleans shortly before DJ's first birthday. But all Bacchus wanted was what Melissa was getting-- pictures, regular visits, phone calls. Bacchus turned out to be Melissa's opposite, smiling easily and quick to laugh.
<v Dan Savage>我们收养DJ时，巴克斯不知道他是父亲，也不知道他的儿子被一对同性恋伴侣收养了。所以在DJ一周岁生日前夕，当巴克斯在纽奥良出现时，我们很紧张。但巴克斯想要的只是梅丽莎得到的——照片、定期访问、电话。巴克斯原来是梅丽莎的对立面，总是面带微笑，爱笑。

00:54:55.750 --> 00:55:10.890
<v Dan Savage>Like most homeless street kids, Melissa works a circuit-- Portland and Seattle in the spring and early summer, Denver, Minneapolis, Chicago, and New York in the late summer, early fall, New Orleans, Phoenix, Las Vegas in the winter. Then it's back up to Portland.
<v Dan Savage>像大多数流浪的街头少年一样，梅丽莎有一条路线——春季和初夏在波特兰和西雅图，夏末初秋在丹佛、明尼阿波利斯、芝加哥和纽约，冬季在纽奥良、凤凰城、拉斯维加斯。然后回到波特兰。

00:55:10.890 --> 00:55:33.880
<v Dan Savage>When Melissa's in Seattle during the summer we all get together in a park where she hangs out with her friends, so that she can visit with DJ and show him off. She keeps in touch by phone the rest of the year, but her calls are usually pretty short. She asks how we're doing. We ask how she's doing. Then we put DJ on the phone.
<v Dan Savage>当梅丽莎夏天在西雅图时，我们都会在公园集合，她和朋友们在一起，这样她可以看望DJ并炫耀他。其余时间她通过电话联系，但她的电话通常很短。她问我们怎么样。我们问她怎么样。然后我们把DJ放上电话。

00:55:33.880 --> 00:55:46.580
<v Dan Savage>The last time Melissa called, she didn't ask for DJ. Her boyfriend, she told me, died the night before of alcohol poisoning. They were sleeping on a sidewalk, he was lying beside her, and when she woke up, he was dead.
<v Dan Savage>上次梅丽莎打电话时，她没问起DJ。她告诉我，她的男友前一天晚上因酒精中毒死了。他们睡在人行道上，他躺在她身边，当她醒来时，他已经死了。

00:55:46.580 --> 00:56:13.780
<v Dan Savage>That call came in early November and we haven't heard from Melissa since. Three months ago, I started calling hospitals, then morgues. When the clerk at the county morgue in New Orleans asked me to describe Melissa, without thinking, I started to say, well, she's kind of quiet. The morgue attendant laughed and told me that all his Jane Does fit that description.
<v Dan Savage>那通电话是在11月初打来的，从那以后我们再没听到梅丽莎的消息。三个月前，我开始给医院打电话，然后是停尸房。当纽奥良县停尸房的职员让我描述梅丽莎时，我没过脑子，刚开始说，嗯，她有点安静。停尸房工作人员笑了，告诉我他那所有的无名女尸都符合那个描述。

00:56:13.780 --> 00:56:35.480
<v Dan Savage>Then just as suddenly, Bacchus stopped calling. Soon, Bacchus's stepmother, who would be DJ's biological step-grandmother, was calling us, too, asking if we knew where Bacchus was. He drifted from New Orleans to Vermont to Texas, she told us, and then disappeared.
<v Dan Savage>然后突然间，巴克斯也不打电话了。很快，巴克斯的继母，也就是DJ的亲生继祖母，也打电话给我们，问我们知不知道巴克斯在哪。她告诉我们，他从纽奥良漂泊到佛蒙特到德克萨斯，然后就消失了。

00:56:35.480 --> 00:56:45.910
<v Dan Savage>We haven't heard from Melissa for six months. Bacchus has been missing for four months now. DJ's birthday came and went, and nothing. No calls.
<v Dan Savage>我们已经六个月没听到梅丽莎的消息了。巴克斯也失踪四个月了。DJ的生日也过了，什么都没有。没电话。

00:56:45.910 --> 00:57:01.970
<v Dan Savage>Last Sunday I was tearing down the wallpaper in my office at home when DJ walked in. His best friend, a little boy named Haven, had spent the night. After Haven's mother picked him up, DJ dragged a stool into my office and sat down to watch me pull wallpaper down in long strips.
<v Dan Savage>上周我正在家里办公室撕壁纸，DJ走了进来。他最好的朋友，一个叫黑文的小男孩，在那过了一夜。黑文的妈妈接走他后，DJ拖了张凳子进我办公室坐下，看着我把壁纸撕成一条条。

00:57:01.970 --> 00:57:17.020
<v Dan Savage>"Have has a mommy," DJ suddenly said. "And I have a mommy." DJ's going through this thing where he likes to make statements of fact and have us confirm them for him. It's as if he's testing himself, making sure his reality jives with ours.
<v Dan Savage>“黑文有妈妈，”DJ突然说。“我也有妈妈。”DJ正经历一个阶段，他喜欢做事实陈述，让我们为他确认。就好像他在测试自己，确保他的现实与我们的相符。

00:57:17.020 --> 00:57:20.690
<v Dan Savage>"That's right," I responded. "You have a money too, just like Haven."
<v Dan Savage>“没错，”我回应道。“你也有妈妈，就像黑文一样。”

00:57:20.690 --> 00:57:28.750
<v Dan Savage>He went on. "My mommy's name is Melissa. I came out of Melissa's tummy. I play with my mommy in the park."
<v Dan Savage>他继续说。“我妈妈叫梅丽莎。我是从梅丽莎肚子里出来的。我和我妈妈在公园玩。”

00:57:28.750 --> 00:57:33.970
<v Dan Savage>Then DJ looked at me and asked a question. "When will I see my mommy again?"
<v Dan Savage>然后DJ看着我问了一个问题。“我什么时候能再见到妈妈？”

00:57:33.970 --> 00:57:41.340
<v Dan Savage>"This summer," I said, hoping it wasn't a lie. "We'll see Melissa in the park this summer, just like last summer."
<v Dan Savage>“今年夏天，”我说，希望这不是个谎言。“我们今年夏天会在公园见到梅丽莎，就像去年夏天一样。”

00:57:41.340 --> 00:58:07.690
<v Dan Savage>I'm not sure what we'll tell DJ if Melissa really has disappeared forever. What if he asks why his mother didn't love him enough to stick around for him? Maybe we'll tell him that some people self-destruct, and maybe Melissa knew she was going to self-destruct and loved him so much that she wanted to make sure he was someplace he wouldn't get hurt. And maybe this answer will be good enough for DJ. And maybe it won't.
<v Dan Savage>我不确定如果梅丽莎真的永远消失了，我们会告诉DJ什么。如果他问为什么他妈妈没爱他到留下来照顾他怎么办？也许我们会告诉他，有些人会自我毁灭，也许梅丽莎知道她会自我毁灭，而且非常爱他，所以她想确保他在一个不会受伤害的地方。也许这个答案对DJ来说足够好。也许不够。

00:58:07.690 --> 00:58:26.000
<v Dan Savage>One of the things my boyfriend brought to our relationship was a video camera. I didn't let him bring it to the hospital with us that day, though. I didn't want a video camera in the room. I wanted to experience those moments, DJ's first moments, in real time, not sitting in front of a TV months later. Maybe I was being selfish.
<v Dan Savage>我男友带进我们关系的东西之一是一台摄像机。那天我不让他带进医院。我不想要一台摄像机在房间里。我想实时体验那些时刻，DJ的第一时刻，而不是几个月后坐在电视前。也许我很自私。

00:58:26.000 --> 00:58:53.430
<v Dan Savage>If Melissa has disappeared forever, well, I deeply regret that we don't have any videotape of DJ with his mother. If he could see her the way we saw her, holding him, feeding him, softly singing to him in her hospital bed the night he was born, he would know what I know, that his mother loved him very much.
<v Dan Savage>如果梅丽莎真的永远消失了，那么，我深感遗憾的是我们没有DJ和他母亲的任何录像。如果他能像我们看到的那样看到她，抱着他，喂他，在他出生那晚在病床上轻声为他唱歌，他就会知道我所知道的，他的母亲非常爱他。

00:58:53.430 --> 00:58:58.430
<v Ira Glass>Dan Savage is the author of The Kid: (What Happened After My Boyfriend and I Decided to Go Get Pregnant).
<v Ira Glass>丹·萨维奇是《孩子：我和男友决定去怀孕后发生了什么》的作者。

00:58:58.430 --> 00:59:18.660
<v Ira Glass>This American Life is distributed by Public Radio International. Alix Spiegel's story was produced with funding from the Corporation for Public Broadcasting. The rap song after her story was written by Travolta W. and performed by him, Kevin G. and Keith L. They are juvenile offenders at Chicago's Audi Home Detention Center as part of a show put on behind bars by Chicago's Music Theatre Workshop.
<v Ira Glass>《美国生活》由国际公共广播电台发行。阿利克斯·斯皮格尔的故事制作经费由公共广播公司资助。她故事后的说唱歌曲由特拉沃尔塔·W创作，由他和凯文·G以及基思·L表演。他们是芝加哥奥迪家庭拘留中心的少年犯，作为芝加哥音乐剧场工作室在狱中上演的节目的一部分。

